WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.840
Linux systems run processes to perform work.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.300
Some processes are temporary, such as a command a user starts and then closes.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:13.040
Others are long-running services, often called daemons, that support login, scheduling,

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.980
logging, networking, databases, web applications, monitoring, backups,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.880
or business applications.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.260
Services are normal, but every service deserves a reason to exist.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.400
Some services listen on network ports and accept connections.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.380
That exposure may be required,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.840
as with a web service on a web server or SSH on an administration path.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.300
But unnecessary listening services create management burden and potential risk.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.760
The issue is not only whether a port is open.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.220
Administrators should know what software owns the port, why it is reachable,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.280
which users or systems need it, how it is updated, and who

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.020
is responsible for it.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.440
Processes and services also include scheduled jobs, local agents, monitoring components,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.300
backup tools, and application workers.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.760
A defensive review asks what is running, whether it should start automatically,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.820
whether it runs with appropriate privilege, where it writes logs, and what

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.560
would happen if it stopped.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.580
Unknown services are not automatically malicious,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.120
but they are operational unknowns that need ownership and documentation.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.860
Changes should be careful.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.040
Disabling a service without understanding dependencies can break applications, monitoring,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.740
remote access, or backups.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.600
A safer approach is to inventory services, confirm purpose with the owner,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.140
test in a non-production environment when possible, plan a change window for

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.160
important systems, and keep a rollback path.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.900
Reducing exposure is valuable, but it should be done deliberately.
